321:\begin{proposition}
323:    Given two area-compatible $k$-district maps and a sequence of area-preserving ReCom moves where districts in intermediate maps are weak polygons with $O(n)$ vertices, we can compute a sequence of area-preserving ReCom moves of the same length where the districts in intermediate maps are all  polygons with $O(n)$ vertices.    
332:    Without loss of generality, $D_1$ is the union of districts not changed in the ReCom that transforms $M_1$ into $M_2$.
341:% \begin{lemma}
342:% Let $M(\mathcal{R}) = \{D_1, D_2, D_3\}$ be a 3-district map with the ordering property on $\mathcal{R} = [0,1]^2$ with complexity $O(n)$.
343:%     Then there is a sequence of $O(\log n)$ area-preserving ReCom moves to the canonical map.
346:% Let $\delta > 0$ be small enough such that each district has no vertices in the region $(0, \delta) \times [0,1] \subseteq \mathcal{R}$. Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be the minimum area of a district of $M$ intersected with $[0,\delta]$. Consider the map $\hat{M}(\hat{\mathcal{R}}) = \{\hat{D}_1, \hat{D}_2, \hat{D}_3\}$ which is the restriction of $M$ to the region $\hat{\mathcal{R}} = [\delta,1] \times [0,1]$. The districts in this map are connected by the choice of $\delta$. Find a sequence of $O(\log n)$ ReCom moves to the canonical configuration on $\hat{\mathcal{R}}$ with areas such that the area deviation relative to $\hat{M}$ at every stage is bounded by $\varepsilon/3$. At each stage in this sequence, the districts intersect the line $\{\delta\} \times [0,1]$ in a connected line segment. Consider the first ReCom move, say between $D_1$ and $D_2$. Since the areas of the new districts $D_1'$ and $D_2'$ differ from their predecessors by at most $\varepsilon/3$, we can extend the second map to a map on all of $\mathcal{R}$ which is area-compatible with the original map $M$. We then repeat this process. There is always enough area among the two recombined districts in the strip $[0, \delta] \times [0,1]$ to make up the deviation in the following step because the area of any district in this strip is at least $2\varepsilon/3$ in any area compatible map in the sequence. We thus obtain a sequence of area-preserving ReCom moves from $M$ to an almost canonical configuration which is the union of a canonical map on $\hat{\mathcal{R}}$ and some simple map on the strip $[0, \delta]$. For sufficiently small $\delta$, three more gravity moves will then move into the canonical map on $\mathcal{R}$.    
351:\subparagraph{Weak Representation for ReCom Sequences.} 
352:We construct a ReComb sequence in two passes: The first pass operates on a generic  $\eps$-perturbation, where the \emph{area} of each district is given by the weak representation (hence the corridors have zero area). The second pass then expands the weak representations into an $\eps$-perturbations, using Proposition~\ref{cor:perturbation}, where each district is a simple polygon with the desired area. Note that the number of moves is determined in the first pass. 
354:%Corollary~\ref{cor:perturbation} shows that we can work with weak representations without loss of generality. We can convert a sequence of area-preserving ReCom moves between two maps, where intermediate maps are represented by weak embeddings, into an area-preserving sequence where every district is a simple polygon. We use this fact in the analysis of our algorithm. 
355:We define the \emph{compressed complexity} of a district map as the number of vertices in the image graph $H$ of the weak representation (that is, repeated vertices are counted only once).
356:The number of ReCom moves produced by our algorithm in Sections~\ref{sec:three}--\ref{sec:general} depends on the compressed complexity. % rather than the regular complexity of the input maps. 
357:Using $\eps$-perturbations would increase the complexity of maps.
366:%We assume that the districts are simple polygons with a total of $n$ vertices. We then reduce the problem with $k$ districts to $3$ districts and show that from there, $O(\log n)$ recombination moves are enough to reach the canonical configuration.
371:In this section, we consider maps with three districts in a unit square domain $\mathcal{R}=[0,1]^2$. We show that any 3-district map $M(\mathcal{R})=\{D_1, D_2,D_3\}$ can be transformed by finite sequence of ReCom moves into an area-compatible \emph{canonical map} in which the districts are axis-aligned rectangles, $Q_1$, $Q_2$ and $Q_3$, of unit width such that $\area(Q_i)=\area(D_i)$ for $i=1,2,3$. 
373:Before describing the algorithm for producing a chain of ReCom moves connecting two maps, we provide a high-level overview of where the main obstacles lie. We can gain some intuition by first considering the case when connectivity is not enforced, i.e., where districts need not be connected. Also assume for simplicity that the areas of the districts are all equal to $\frac{1}{3}$. In this setting, three ReCom moves suffice. Indeed, recombine and split $D_1$ and $D_2$ so that the new $D_1$ does not intersect $Q_3$ (this is always possible since the area of $D_3 \cup Q_3$ is at most $\frac{2}{3}$). We can now recombine $D_2$ and $D_3$ so that the new $D_3$ equals $Q_3$, and the third ReCom move can complete the transformation. Let us now return to the case where the district must remain connected. The first main insight shown in this section is that the three-step process above can still be applied, just by adding narrow corridors to maintain connectivity at each stage. However, to reach canonical form, we must remove these corridors, which requires care. The main difficulty here comes from the fact that corridors usually run alongside each other. The removal of any such corridor would require at least two ReCom moves. The second insight is a method to remove such corridors suing a number of ReCom moves bounded by a function of the complexity of the initial map.
378:Our algorithm for transforming a map into the canonical map consists of three stages, each containing multiple ReCom moves:
381:    \item \emph{Gravity moves} (Section \ref{ssec:gravity}). We perform three ReCom moves to place the districts into their final positions, with the possible exception of corridors. Moves needed: $3$.
384:% After some preprocessing moves, our first objective is to move the districts into their final positions, with the possible exception of corridors. This can be accomplished in three ReCom moves ($\gravity$ moves). After that, our objective is to eliminate corridors, eventually removing all of them in $O(\log n)$ ReCom moves ($\exchange$ sequences). $\gravity$ moves and the  $\exchange$ sequences are described in Section~\ref{ssec:gravity} and \ref{ssec:exchange} below.
391:First we transform the three given districts into simple polygons if necessary. While there is a district $D_i$ that is a polygon with holes, there is an adjacent district $D_j$ contained within a hole. Recombine $D_i$ and $D_j$ to create a single-edge corridor between $D_j$ and the outer boundary of $D_i$. Next, we create corridors, if necessary, such that each district touches both the left and right sides of $\mathcal{R}$. While there is a district that is not adjacent to the left (resp., right) side $s$ of the $\mathcal{R}$, let $D_i$ be such a district closest to $s$ and let $D_j$ be an adjacent district that already touches $s$; then we recombine $D_i$ and $D_j$ and append to $D_i$ a shortest path to $s$ along the boundary of $D_j$. Thus, both districts remain simply connected. As all corridors run along existing vertices of the three districts, the complexity of the map does not increase. This stage takes $O(1)$ ReCom moves.
397:We also need to establish the property that the middle district has the largest area. This can be done trivially with a single ReCom move between the middle district and the largest district of the three. We call these properties combined the \emph{ordering property}:
