\subparagraph{Weak Representation for ReCom Sequences.} 
We construct a ReComb sequence in two passes: The first pass operates on a generic  $\eps$-perturbation, where the \emph{area} of each district is given by the weak representation (hence the corridors have zero area). The second pass then expands the weak representations into an $\eps$-perturbations, using Proposition~\ref{cor:perturbation}, where each district is a simple polygon with the desired area. Note that the number of moves is determined in the first pass. 

%Corollary~\ref{cor:perturbation} shows that we can work with weak representations without loss of generality. We can convert a sequence of area-preserving ReCom moves between two maps, where intermediate maps are represented by weak embeddings, into an area-preserving sequence where every district is a simple polygon. We use this fact in the analysis of our algorithm. 
We define the \emph{compressed complexity} of a district map as the number of vertices in the image graph $H$ of the weak representation (that is, repeated vertices are counted only once).
The number of ReCom moves produced by our algorithm in Sections~\ref{sec:three}--\ref{sec:general} depends on the compressed complexity. % rather than the regular complexity of the input maps. 
Using $\eps$-perturbations would increase the complexity of maps.
For this reason, it is also useful in our analysis to convert an $\eps$-perturbations to a weak embedding which we do by applying the inverse of the operations described here.
Throughout this paper we use set operations on weak polygons such as $D_1\cup D_2$ where $D_1$ and $D_2$ are weak polygons.
Let $D_1'$ and $D_2'$ be the polygons obtained by the $\eps$-perturbation defined in Proposition~\ref{cor:perturbation}.
We define $D_1\cup D_2$ to be the weak polygon obtained from $D_1'\cup D_2'$.


